// T4 SOUNDNESS gate: a value reached through a PACKAGE-LEVEL GLOBAL must never
// be released.
//
// `call_result_is_owned` approves a Goo call whose return_escapes is false, and
// that summary tracks PARAMETERS ONLY. A function returning a global therefore
// looks identical to one returning a fresh allocation, so the caller's local is
// approved and the global's object is freed out from under every later use.
//
// Measured before the fix: exit 2, "panic: release of an object with reference
// count 0", and two Invalid reads on the SECOND call.
//
// BOTH SHAPES, because the defect is not about errors. The error form is what
// PR #275 made reachable; the bare-pointer form has been reachable since #261
// and is the reason the fix belongs at the global, not in the error arm.
//
// A THIRD SHAPE (fix round 1, Task 2b Critical): a global initialized from a
// STRING LITERAL, then aliased into a second global (`msgA` / `msgB` below).
// codegen_const_string_value emits a literal's header as a CONSTANT PRIVATE
// GLOBAL whose rc field is ALREADY GOO_RC_IMMORTAL at compile time, so it
// lands in .rodata. Before the fix-round-1 read-before-write guard,
// goo_make_immortal stored into that read-only memory unconditionally and
// crashed with SIGSEGV -- measured on the four-line reproduction
// `var a = "hello"; var b = a; func main() { println(b) }`, exit 139. Unlike
// the other two shapes, this one fails at PROGRAM INIT, before main ever
// runs, so it needs no loop to reach.
package main

import "errors"

var ErrSentinel error = errors.New("sentinel failure")
var GCounter *int = new(int)
var msgA = "hello from a global"
var msgB = msgA

var sink int

func getErr() error { return ErrSentinel }
func getPtr() *int  { return GCounter }

func useErr() int {
    e := getErr()
    if errors.Is(e, e) {
        return 1
    }
    return 0
}

func usePtr() int {
    p := getPtr()
    if p == nil {
        return 0
    }
    return 1
}

func main() {
    total := 0
    // MORE THAN ONE CALL IS THE WHOLE POINT. The first call frees the global;
    // the second is the one that reads freed memory. A single-call probe
    // reports clean against the exact defect it exists to catch.
    for i := 0; i < 100; i++ {
        total = total + useErr()
        total = total + usePtr()
    }
    sink = total
    println(total)
    println(msgB)
}
